[38;5;252m[1m## Two real bugs found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10msrc/dfa_match.rs[0m — caseless repeat opcodes misclassified.[22m Five match arms used [38;5;10mcodevalue >= OP_STARI[0m to detect the caseless [38;5;10mI[0m variants. But the opcode numbering is [38;5;10mOP_STAR..OP_POSUPTO[0m (33–45), [38;5;10mOP_STARI..OP_POSUPTOI[0m (46–58), [38;5;10mOP_NOTSTAR..OP_NOTPOSUPTO[0m (59–71), [38;5;10mOP_NOTSTARI..OP_NOTPOSUPTOI[0m (72–84) — so every [3mcaseful[23m [38;5;10mOP_NOT*[0m opcode also passed the test and had 13 subtracted from [38;5;10mcodevalue[0m, landing in the wrong range. For [38;5;10m[^a]*a[0m against [38;5;10m"a"[0m, [38;5;10mOP_NOTPOSSTAR[0m (68) became [38;5;10mOP_POSSTARI[0m (55), which inverted the [38;5;10mcodevalue < OP_NOTSTAR[0m sense and skipped the possessive [38;5;10mactive_count--[0m, so the DFA added a bogus "repeat continues" state. Replaced with an [38;5;10mis_caseless_repeat()[0m helper testing both caseless ranges. (In [38;5;10mmatch_.rs[0m the same [38;5;10m>= OP_STARI[0m test is correct, because [38;5;10mREPEATCHAR[0m is only reached from the non-[38;5;10mNOT[0m opcodes — C does the same thing there.)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10msrc/convert.rs[0m — [38;5;10mconvert_posix[0m ignored the UTF flag.[22m The parameter was named [38;5;10m_utf[0m and the code called [38;5;10mgetcharlentest(posix, true)[0m, always decoding UTF-8. In non-UTF mode C reads one code unit at a time, so multi-byte sequences were consumed as a single character: [38;5;10mclength[0m differed, which changed the overflow accounting and (for inputs where a continuation byte is a metacharacter) would change the conversion itself.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Both were caught only because the tests compare intermediate state rather than just return values — the DFA workspace (which holds the live active/new state lists, so it validates the automaton step by step) and the partial contents of an undersized output buffer.[0m[0m
